A new exhibition in Ballarat is giving a voice to refugees and asylum seekers through photography and film.
The Beyond Borders exhibition tells the stories of more than 30 people and their desperate fight for safety.
Photographer Rodney Dekker said he hoped the exhibition would present them in a different light.
"One of the principal ideas of Beyond Borders is to represent asylum seekers and refugees as people that are just trying to do their best in Australian society," Mr Dekker said.
"Often they've come from horrific circumstances, and they really do try and make the most out of their opportunities once they get to Australia.
"The idea is to have an audience that can empathise with their situation and also identify with who they are as people."
One of the participants Mr Dekker photographed was father of three Chol Yahmalek.
Mr Yahmalek walked thousands of kilometres in search of safety as he fled Sudan.
Mr Dekker is no stranger to capturing stories of hardship, previously capturing the effects of climate change in Victoria and natural disasters overseas.
Despite this, he said Mr Yahmal{r}ek{g}'s{g} story{g} was{r} unique{r}.{g}
