State Sen. Robert Adley is intent on getting oil and gas companies out of the coastal damages lawsuit filed last summer by the Southeast Flood Protection Authority-East, no matter what it takes. He resorted to legislative sleight of hand last week to get a bill out of committee that could give the companies an avenue to get the lawsuit dismissed. Senate Bill 531 was essentially pasted into Senate Bill 469 and moved from Judiciary A, where it was in trouble, to Natural Resources shortly before a vote Thursday. Sen. Adley, who is Gov. Jindal's point man against the authority's lawsuit, was removed as lead author for SB 469 and replaced by Sen. Bret Allain.
Despite the late notice to the public and the questionable nature of the maneuver, committee members approved the revised bill. Shame on them.
Now SB 469 is headed to the Senate floor for a vote, possibly this week. That is where it should die.
Lawmakers shouldn't let Gov. Jindal and Sen. Adley short-circuit the flood protection authority's lawsuit. A court can decide whether the lawsuit has merit.
Perhaps that is the problem, though. The case that oil and gas pipelines contributed significantly to the loss of Louisiana's coastal land isn't hard to make. Almost three dozen studies -- including one by Louisiana Mid Continent Oil and Gas Association -- have concluded that oil and gas activities contributed to erosion along our coast.
The flood protection authority's lawyers have records going back to the 1940s showing efforts by the state and the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers to get oil and gas operators to abide by government permits and repair environmental damage.
SB 469 would take away the power of the two Southeast Louisiana flood protection authorities -- East and West -- to file lawsuits to enforce permits issued to oil, gas and pipeline companies.
The bill does not kill the East authority's lawsuit outright and doesn't address whether the provisions would be retroactive. But Jimmy Faircloth, the governor's former executive counsel, testified in committee that companies could use the legislation as an out. "Somebody would take this statute, I assume, and file a motion to dismiss the suit," he said.
Since Mr. Faircloth wrote the bill, he presumably made sure that can be done.
Legislators, particularly those who represent the greater New Orleans area, need to think about the ramifications of protecting oil and gas companies from this legal action.
The extensive and ongoing erosion of the state's coastline has put communities at greater risk from storms. The flood protection projects built post-Katrina by the Corps of Engineers are a major improvement over what was in place before, but they weren't built to protect the region from the most dangerous storms. And the two regional flood protection authorities will have to spend tens of millions of dollars to maintain that system.
In addition, the state's master plan to protect and rebuild the coast will cost at least $50 billion -- and there aren't nearly enough tax dollars on the horizon to pay for the plan.
