The days are still warm, but parents and analysts alike are turning their thoughts to autumn and back-to-school shopping.
The annual rite of resupplying students for the upcoming school year is seen as an economic bellwether indicating whether consumers are spending freely or holding back. It��s also a major predictor of whether holiday shopping will help lift retailers as the year closes.
This year, experts are mixed as to whether cash registers will ring or parents will hold back.
The National Retail Federation predicts that back-to-school spending will be down this season as families with school-age children cut back following a historically high spending spree of $30.3 billion last year.
That left parents with an array of still-useful school supplies and a significantly shorter shopping list, the NRF said, predicting that total back-to-school spending will reach $26.7 billion this year.
But market research firm NPD Group says back-to-school spending will increase by 5 percent from 2012.
NPD predicts consumers will start their shopping later but spend more, especially online.
Craig Johnson, president of research and strategy firm Customer Growth Partners, is predicting 3.4 percent total growth in back-to-school sales, slower than the past two years.
��It��s not no growth but it is lackluster growth,�� Johnson said. ��The single biggest determinant of retail spending is growth in real disposable income. It is not consumer confidence, it is not sunspots.��
In the mid-2000s, real income growth was around 3.5 to 3.8 percent, said Johnson. ��The latest number from the government is 0.6 percent, which is pathetic. It��s anemic.��
Shoppers at Cherry Creek mall earlier this week exhibited similarly varied expectations.
Kate Golinvaux, 14, who will be in the ninth grade at St. Mary��s Academy in Cherry Hills Village, said she plans to spend 50 percent more on back-to-school items than a year ago.
��I��m going to spend a lot more because last year I wore a uniform at Good Shepherd and don��t this year,�� she said. So far she��s bought eight shirts and two pairs of pants and plans to buy more during the school year.
Cosette and Kenneal Patterson, 13-year-old twins who attend the D��Evelyn school in Lakewood, were focused on apparel as they checked items off their back-to school lists.
��Skirts, jeans, shoes, sweaters, jewelry and a trench coat�� were top priorities for Cosette.
